sections.body:
Ignoring the strongest objection makes an argument look naive or dishonest. Addressing it:

- builds **ethos** by showing fairness and knowledge;
- **preempts** objections the audience is already thinking;
- lets the writer **reframe** the debate on better terms.

The key is choosing the **strongest** version of the opposing view (sometimes called steelmanning), not a weak caricature (a **straw man**). Refuting a straw man persuades no one who actually holds the view.
